 # Turbulence Function
 
-Also called: Improved Perlin Noise, M-Noise, Modified Noise, Procedural Noise for Micro-level Details, Turbulence
+Also called: Improved Perlin Noise for Fire, Micro Fire Effects via Noise, Modified Noise, Procedural Noise for Micro-level Details, Turbulence, Improved Perlin Noise, M-Noise
 
 ## Overview
-The Turbulence Function is a procedural noise technique used to generate complex, fractal-like micro-details for volumetric effects such as fire, smoke, and fog [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It works by summing multiple octaves of a base gradient noise function (typically M-Noise or Improved Perlin Noise) with increasing frequency and decreasing amplitude [S2][S3, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its parent, Fractal Brownian Motion (fBm), by its specific application to simulating natural fractal phenomena in real-time graphics, often utilizing M-Noise for its GPU efficiency [S2][S3, unofficial].
+The Turbulence Function is a procedural noise technique that generates complex, fractal-like micro-details for volumetric effects such as fire, smoke, and fog [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It works by summing multiple octaves of a base gradient noise function with increasing frequency and decreasing amplitude [S2][S3, unofficial]. It builds on Fractal Brownian Motion (fBm) by specifically applying summed noise octaves to simulate natural fractal phenomena in real-time graphics [S2][S3, unofficial].
 
